---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/SectionHead.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import { drills, jhookSteps, jhookMistakes, survival } from "../data/flying";
---

<Base
  title="Flying and the J-hook"
  description="A nine-drill WARDOGS practice syllabus for the firing range, the J-hook broken into six inputs, the mistakes that ruin it, and what actually gets helicopters shot down."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Airmanship"
    title="Flying, and the"
    accent="J-hook."
    lede="All of this happens in the Firing Range and none of it happens in a match. Nobody wants to lose an $8,000 loadout because a stranger crashed on takeoff."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <SectionHead
      eyebrow="Syllabus"
      title="Nine drills, in order."
      lede="Each one assumes the one before it. Go to the Firing Range from the bottom-left of the main menu; forward from spawn and two sharp lefts to the vehicle vendor, then Air. Everything there is free."
    />

    <ol class="steps">
      {
        drills.map((d) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <b>{d.title}</b>
              <span>{d.detail}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionHead
      eyebrow="The combat landing"
      title="The J-hook."
      lede="Coming in slowly nose-up and riding the collective down takes forever, which makes you a stationary target. Done properly a J-hook takes you from 300 km/h to about 70 km/h in one manoeuvre, without gaining altitude."
    />

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card-eyebrow">The inputs, in order</span>
        <ol class="tight">
          {jhookSteps.map((s) => <li set:html={s} />)}
        </ol>
      </article>

      <div class="stack-tight">
        <Callout label="Why it works" critical>
          <p>
            Banking hard spills the lift vector sideways, so instead of the energy
            going into a climb — which is what happens if you just pull the nose
            up — it goes into the turn. That is the whole trick:
            <strong>the turn is the airbrake.</strong>
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-eyebrow">The mistakes</span>
          <ul class="tight">
            {jhookMistakes.map((m) => <li set:html={m} />)}
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionHead
      eyebrow="Before you trust it"
      title="The unglamorous version."
    />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Before you trust a J-hook with six people on board, there is a simpler
        deceleration that works: <strong>pitch the nose up and drop the
        collective all the way down</strong>, and the aircraft glides toward the
        zone shedding speed. It has one requirement — <strong>start it far
        out</strong>. Almost every recorded mistake is the same one: not pitching
        up soon enough, carrying too much speed into the zone, and having to go
        around while people shoot at you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Callout label="Low hover">
      <p>
        Once you are near the ground, <strong>make tiny inputs</strong>. Big
        corrections at low speed set up an oscillation — the aircraft rocks back
        and forth, and each correction makes the next one worse. And if you start
        taking fire on short final, land anyway: panicking is what turns a hit
        into a crash.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionHead
      eyebrow="Where to put it down"
      title="The manoeuvre rarely kills you. The spot does."
    />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Check the map before takeoff and again in the air, follow your team's
        movement, and <strong>land behind where they have just cleared</strong>.
        Never an open field: put the airframe between structures, in a confined
        space, with cover on more than one side. The same technique lands on top
        of towers, which is how squads take FOBs from the roof down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionHead eyebrow="Survival" title="Not getting shot down." />
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        {survival.map((s) => <li set:html={s} />)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="pager" aria-label="Next">
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/logistics/">Next: logistics</a>
  </nav>
</Base>

<style>
  .card .card-eyebrow { display: block; }
  ol.tight, ul.tight {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm);
  }
  ol.tight li, ul.tight li { margin-bottom: var(--space-xs); }
  ol.tight li::marker, ul.tight li::marker { color: var(--color-hairline); }
  .tight :global(strong) { color: var(--color-foreground); font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); }
  .tight :global(em) { font-style: normal; color: var(--color-lumen); }
  .stack-tight > * + * { margin-top: var(--space-md); }
  .pager { display: flex; justify-content: flex-end; padding-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
